[36m(TaskRunner pid=592655)[0m code='\nThe `lps` function calculates the length of the longest palindromic subsequence (LPS) in a given string using dynamic programming. It uses a 2D array `dp` where `dp[i][j]` represents the length of the LPS in the substring `s[i...j]`. The algorithm iterates from the end of the string to the beginning, updating the `dp` table based on whether the characters at the ends are equal or not.\n\n**Key Points:**\n1. **Dynamic Programming Approach:** Uses a 2D array to store intermediate results.\n2. **Base Case:** When `i == j`, the LPS length is 1 (since a single character is a palindrome).\n3. **Recurrence Relation:**\n   - If `s[i] == s[j]`, then `dp[i][j] = 2 + dp[i+1][j-1]`.\n   - If `s[i] != s[j]`, then `dp[i][j] = max(dp[i+1][j], dp[i][j-1])`.\n4. **Final Result:** The value at `dp[0][n-1]` gives the length of the LPS for the entire string.\n\nThis approach ensures an efficient solution with a time complexity of O(n²), where n is the length of the input string.\n'
